## Windows shell notes

`powershell` is enabled here, alongside `bash` (Git Bash / MSYS).

### A stray `nul` file is created by `>nul` in the bash tool

`2>nul` / `>nul` make MSYS create a **real 0-byte file named `nul`** in the
current directory. It is then painful to delete: `del nul` and
`Remove-Item nul` resolve to the NUL *device*, not to the file, and PowerShell
reports success while the file stays. Only these work:

- Git Bash: `rm -- ./nul`
- cmd / PowerShell, with the extended-length prefix:
  `Remove-Item -LiteralPath '\\?\C:\path\to\nul'`

### `$PROFILE` is not loaded

The tool runs with `-NoProfile`, so aliases and functions defined in your
PowerShell profile (for example `gdf`, `gst`, `serve`, `open`, `here`,
`neofetch`, `vim`) do not exist inside it. Use real command names, and prefer
cmdlets over aliases so output stays predictable:

- `Get-ChildItem` instead of `ls`
- `Remove-Item -Recurse -Force` instead of `rm -rf`
- `Remove-Item -LiteralPath` for paths with special characters
- the `\\?\` extended prefix for reserved device names (`nul`, `con`, `aux`, `prn`)
